Every object sits at an **address**, a numbered place in memory. `&qty` gives `qty`'s address, and `int* p` holds one. Addresses here are made up to keep them short.

```cpp
int qty = 300;
int* p = &qty;
*p = 500;
std::cout << qty << "\n";
```

A pointer can hold the address of a vector element. Adding a whole number moves it along the elements.

```cpp
std::vector<double> px{101.50, 101.25, 101.75, 102.00};
double* p = &px[1];
```

